WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.960
All right guys, so here is example number two

00:02.960 --> 00:04.760
for breaker block entries.

00:04.760 --> 00:07.520
So you can see we have higher high, higher low

00:07.520 --> 00:09.240
and higher high.

00:09.240 --> 00:10.640
And then we have a

00:13.360 --> 00:15.920
a market structure shift right here.

00:15.920 --> 00:20.440
So normally what you would do is you would take your entry

00:20.440 --> 00:23.960
from this favorite gap or this favorite gap.

00:23.960 --> 00:27.000
But if you are using the breaker block entry model,

00:28.000 --> 00:29.960
your breaker block is right here,

00:29.960 --> 00:32.800
which is the last candle before the move to the upside.

00:32.800 --> 00:36.840
And after that you have a shift in market structure.

00:36.840 --> 00:39.360
So you see this move, right?

00:39.360 --> 00:43.640
There was no bearish candle around here, okay?

00:43.640 --> 00:44.800
If there was a bearish,

00:44.800 --> 00:49.040
let's assume that this candle was bearish, right?

00:49.040 --> 00:50.360
And then you had a,

00:50.360 --> 00:53.880
so I would consider then this as market structure shift.

00:53.960 --> 00:57.240
And then I would consider that candle as my breaker

00:57.240 --> 01:01.520
because apparently that was the last bearish candle

01:02.720 --> 01:06.560
or last candle or you could say high, high, high low,

01:06.560 --> 01:09.600
last high low and then we had higher high

01:09.600 --> 01:11.000
and then market shift.

01:11.000 --> 01:14.360
So I hope you get what I'm trying to explain.

01:14.360 --> 01:18.480
So, but in this case, this was the last candle, right?

01:18.480 --> 01:22.240
Or sometimes what I would do is it's sometimes

01:22.240 --> 01:26.120
a good practice to mark out the bearish candle

01:26.120 --> 01:29.640
instead of the bullish candle and vice versa.

01:29.640 --> 01:32.400
So if you are looking for a long position,

01:32.400 --> 01:34.480
you should mark out the bullish candle

01:34.480 --> 01:36.720
instead of the bearish candle.

01:36.720 --> 01:39.160
But it's not gonna affect that much.

01:39.160 --> 01:42.840
You can take your entry from the last candle, okay?

01:42.840 --> 01:44.000
Or the, yeah.

01:44.000 --> 01:47.720
So you can see if I drag this along,

01:47.720 --> 01:49.400
we had market structure shift

01:49.400 --> 01:52.800
and what was really nice about this breaker block

01:52.800 --> 01:55.280
is that we had fair value gap here as well.

01:56.160 --> 02:00.440
Now that is when breaker blocks become very, very,

02:00.440 --> 02:02.560
I would say powerful, okay?

02:02.560 --> 02:05.080
So keep that in mind that yeah,

02:05.080 --> 02:09.600
you can use breakers as their own without fair value gap,

02:09.600 --> 02:11.040
but if there's fair value gap,

02:11.040 --> 02:13.800
then that entry is gonna be perfect.

02:13.800 --> 02:16.760
So I'm gonna explain another thing.

02:16.760 --> 02:19.280
A lot of people say that they missed their entries

02:19.280 --> 02:22.760
whenever they tried to enter from the extreme fair value gap.

02:22.760 --> 02:25.920
If you are, if you don't wanna miss, you know,

02:25.920 --> 02:28.440
the trades, what you can do is right

02:28.440 --> 02:30.800
when you have a market structure shift,

02:30.800 --> 02:34.920
you can take your entry from the very recent fair value gap,

02:34.920 --> 02:35.760
okay?

02:35.760 --> 02:37.280
Instead of taking your entry from the extreme,

02:37.280 --> 02:39.480
you can take your entry from the very first,

02:39.480 --> 02:41.920
but there's a catch, okay?

02:41.920 --> 02:44.680
You need to secure your stop loss.

02:44.680 --> 02:48.760
So let's say if fries goes back to the extreme fair value gap,

02:48.760 --> 02:50.240
you do not lose, okay?

02:50.240 --> 02:52.280
You do not wanna give your money to the markets,

02:52.280 --> 02:54.640
even though your analysis are right.

02:55.840 --> 02:58.280
So this is the only way, guys.

02:58.280 --> 03:01.720
If you want me to tell you guys to put your stop here,

03:01.720 --> 03:06.280
yeah, of course you can, but you're gonna lose, okay?

03:06.280 --> 03:10.480
And even though your analysis are going to be perfect,

03:10.480 --> 03:13.120
a price can stop you up because it needs to fill

03:13.120 --> 03:15.360
and balance the prices up there.

03:15.360 --> 03:18.520
So keep your stop maybe here or maybe here,

03:18.520 --> 03:20.640
and kaboom, okay?

03:23.160 --> 03:24.000
Perfect.

03:24.000 --> 03:28.360
So yeah, guys, I really hope that the,

03:31.000 --> 03:32.360
you know, I really hope that you guys

03:32.360 --> 03:35.040
have a clear knowledge about breaker blocks now.

03:35.040 --> 03:37.680
It's simply the last candle.

03:37.680 --> 03:41.560
So you have, so see, I'll try to explain it once again.

03:41.560 --> 03:44.240
If Justin cares if anyone did understand.

03:44.240 --> 03:48.280
So you have higher high, higher low, higher high.

03:49.520 --> 03:50.400
Right?

03:50.400 --> 03:53.320
So the last candle of the higher low

03:55.680 --> 03:57.280
is gonna be your breaker block.

03:58.280 --> 04:02.880
And you must get a market structure shift

04:02.880 --> 04:06.360
after this new high is created, right?

04:07.280 --> 04:08.840
And when you have market structure shift,

04:08.840 --> 04:12.320
then that breaker block becomes valid.

04:12.320 --> 04:16.560
However, if you had, let's say, higher high, higher low,

04:16.560 --> 04:19.480
higher high, it's small, higher low,

04:19.480 --> 04:22.400
and then higher high, and then market structure shift,

04:22.400 --> 04:25.760
then that last candle would be your breaker.

04:25.760 --> 04:27.960
So yeah, let's move on to the next videos.

